  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how nuclear pore complexes (NPCs) in the nuclear envelope can differ in composition and how that diversity may shape transport and genome organization inside the nucleus. Across the top, three nuclei are shown with distinct classes of pores, each apparently favoring different transport proteins or cargo types, suggesting that not all pores are functionally identical. The middle row expands this idea by showing that nuclei containing different pore types are associated with different mixtures or arrangements of internal chromatin domains.\n\nThe bottom row summarizes two linked roles. On the left, differently composed NPCs mediate nuclear transport, including movement of transport factors and export of an mRNA-containing complex. On the right, the same heterogeneity is connected to chromosome arrangement within the nucleus and to regulation of gene expression, with colored chromosome regions positioned differently relative to the nuclear periphery.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a model in which NPCs are not uniform channels but specialized structures. Their molecular composition appears to influence which molecules pass through them and how chromosomal regions are organized, thereby linking transport at the nuclear envelope to control of gene activity.",
